@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Every question widget, one example each)

Every block on this page is live in the preview. Each feature gets one line and one working example; the block is also what prints in the PDF.

@section(Multiple choice, one answer)

Ask a question with several options where exactly one is right; the student picks it with a click.

Which metal conducts best? @q(mcv: *silver, iron, lead, tin)

@b{Three layouts.} mch puts the options in a row, mcv stacks them, mcd makes a dropdown that sits inside a sentence.

The SI unit of force is the @q(mcd: *newton, joule, watt).

@b{The right option.} A star in front of an option marks it right.

2 + 2 = @q(mch: 3, *4, 5)

@b{Partly right options.} A tilde gives part credit: ~ alone is half, ~0.3 is 30%.

Best first step? @q(mcv: *measure it, ~0.5 estimate it, guess)

@b{Columns.} cols=N lays the options out in N columns instead of one tall stack.

Pick the prime @q(mcv, cols=2, *7, 8, 9, 10)

@b{Points and shuffling.} pts= sets the points (1 if you leave it out).

Pick the noble gas @q(mcv, pts=2, shuffle, *neon, sodium, iron, =none of these)

@b{Option labels on paper.} labels=letters (the default), numbers or none decides how options are labelled.

Pick one @q(mcv, labels=numbers, *a, b, c)

@b{Graded.} One mark for the starred option, part credit for a ~ option, 0 otherwise.

@section(Check all that apply)

Ask a question where any number of options may be right; the student ticks every one that applies.

Pick every prime: @q(mah: *2, *3, 4, *5, 9)

@b{Three layouts.} mah is a row of checkboxes, mav a stack, mad a list you can pick several from.

Which are vectors? @q(mav: *velocity, speed, *force, mass)

@b{How part credit is counted.} grading= picks the rule: partial (the default: right ticks earn, wrong ticks cost), proportional (wrong ticks are ignored), jaccard (overlap of the two sets), allornothing.

Pick the metals @q(mah, grading=allornothing, *iron, *copper, glass)

@b{The cost of a wrong tick.} penalty= sets how much a wrong tick takes away, from 0 up.

Pick the even numbers @q(mah, penalty=0.5, *2, *4, 5)

@b{Partly right options.} ~ (or ~0.3) makes an option worth part of a right one.

Good safety habits @q(mav: *wear goggles, ~0.5 read the label, run in the lab)

@b{Graded.} Under the default partial rule each right tick earns its share and each wrong tick loses penalty= of a share, never going below 0.

@section(Fill in the blank: text answers)

Leave a blank in a sentence for the student to type a word or phrase into, marked against the answers you list.

The capital of France is @q(f: Paris).

@b{Several right answers.} Separate accepted answers with | or commas; any one is full credit.

The unit of power is the @q(f: watt|watts|W).

@b{Case and spaces.} Case is ignored by default.

Element symbol for sodium: @q(fcs: Na). Two words: @q(fsi: New York)

@b{A blank in the middle of a sentence.} @@q answer @@e puts the answer inline without the colon form.

The sky is @q blue @e today.

@b{Box size.} f72 makes the box 72 points wide; f72,30 also 30 tall.

Name: @q(f200: x)

@b{Part credit for near misses.} partial= lists answers worth less: value:credit, separated by |.

Capital of Australia @q(f, partial=sydney:0.25, Canberra)

@b{Accepted exceptions.} except= names an @@exceptionset of extra words that are always accepted, ignoring case and spaces.

Unit of charge @q(f: coulomb, C)

@b{Graded.} The typed answer is compared with each accepted answer, ignoring case (unless fcs) and surrounding spaces; a match is full credit, a partial= entry its share.

@section(Fill in with a pattern)

Accept any answer that matches a pattern, for spelling variants and answers with many correct shapes.

Spell the colour: @q(fr: colou?r)

@b{Alternatives.} a|b accepts either.

Zero: @q(fr: zero|0)

@b{Optional and repeated parts.} ? makes the letter before it optional, + allows it once or more, [0-9] is any digit.

A year in the 1900s: @q(fr: 19[0-9][0-9])

@b{Case.} Matching ignores case unless you write fr.cs.

@q(fr.cs: NaCl)

@b{Graded.} The whole answer must match the pattern.

@section(Number answers)

Ask for a number and accept anything within a tolerance or range, so 3.14 and 3.142 are both right.

Pi to two decimal places: @q(fn: 3.13, 3.15)

@b{A range or a value.} fn: lo, hi accepts anything between.

@tolerance(2)
g on Earth, in m/s^2: @q(fn: 9.81)

@b{Expressions.} The student may type pi/2 or 1/3; it is worked out before comparing.

Half of pi: @q(fn: 1.570, 1.571)

@b{Thresholds.} >20, >=20 and <5 accept any number past the limit.

Any number above ten: @q(fn: >10)

@b{Whole numbers only.} n rejects 3.5; n, 1, 10 is an integer in a range.

Sides of a hexagon: @q(n: 6)

@b{Computed answers.} With @@rand values, fn=expression works out each student's own answer.

@rand(R, 1:10)
Current through @var(R) ohms at 10 V, in amps: @q(fn=10/R, 2)

@b{Complex numbers.} fc, re, im, tol: the student may type 3+4j or 5*exp(i*pi/6).

Impedance: @q(fc, 3, 4, 0.01)

@b{Graded.} The typed value is evaluated (so 1/3 and 0.3333 both work) and compared with the range or tolerance; inside is full credit.

@section(Interval answers)

Ask for an interval, such as the range a value lies in; the student types something like 3-5 or 3 to 5.

Normal body temperature, in degrees Celsius: @q(range, 36, 37.5)

@b{How intervals are compared.} mode=overlap accepts any interval that overlaps yours, contains one that contains it, within one inside it.

A range inside 10 to 20: @q(range, 10, 20, mode=within)

@b{Slack.} tol= allows the ends to be off by that much.

@q(range, 3, 5, tol=0.5)

@b{Graded.} The two ends the student types are compared with yours under mode= and tol=; right or wrong.

@section(Equation and formula answers)

Ask for a formula and accept any form that is mathematically the same, so x+1 and 1+x are both right.

Expand (x+1)^2: @q(eq: x^2 + 2x + 1)

@b{Equivalent forms.} The answer is tried at many random points, so any equal expression is right.

Simplify 2(a+b): @q(eq: 2a + 2b)

@b{Must be factored, expanded or reduced.} eq.factored rejects the multiplied-out form, eq.expanded the product, eq.reduced a fraction not in lowest terms.

Factor x^2 - 1: @q(eq.factored: (x-1)(x+1))

@b{Exactly as written.} eq.strict compares the text only, with no algebra.

@q(eq.strict: x+1)

@b{The simplest Boolean form.} eq.min wants a minimal sum of products (a K-map answer): any equally small cover is right, a longer equivalent is not.

Minimise: @q(eq.min: A'B + AB')

@b{A formula box with a preview.} formula draws the student's typing as maths underneath as they type.

Ohm's law for current: @q(formula: V/R)

@b{Graded.} Right when the student's expression equals the key at every sampled point (and, for factored/expanded/reduced, also has that shape).

@section(Name several, in any order)

Ask the student to list several things in one blank, in any order, with part credit for each one right.

Name the colours of the French flag: @q(unordered: blue, white, red)

@b{Name any N of a pool.} uo3 accepts any three members of a longer list; naming more never hurts.

Name three chip makers: @q(uo3: AMD, Intel, Nvidia, TSMC, Samsung)

@b{Alternative spellings.} Each member may list alternatives with |.

@q(uo: red|rouge, white|blanc, blue|bleu)

@b{Graded.} Each right member earns its share (of all members, or of N for uoN); duplicates count once.

@section(Slider answers)

Let the student answer by dragging a slider to a value, marked within a tolerance.

Set the angle that gives the longest throw: @q(slider, min=0, max=90, answer=45, tol=3)

@b{Range and step.} min=, max= and step= set the scale.

@q(slider, min=0, max=1, step=0.05, answer=0.5, tol=0.05)

@b{Tolerance.} tol= is how far from answer= still counts.

@q(slider, min=0, max=100, answer=42, tol=2)

@b{Graded.} Right when the value is within tol= of answer=.

@section(Matching pairs)

Give a list of terms and ask the student to match each to its partner from a shuffled list.

@q(match, H=hydrogen, O=oxygen, N=nitrogen)

@b{Distractors.} An entry with no = is an extra wrong choice.

@q(match, Fe=iron, Cu=copper, tin, zinc)

@b{Columns.} cols=N lays the pairs out in N columns.

@q(match, cols=2, a=1, b=2, c=3, d=4)

@b{Graded.} Each correct pair earns its share of the points.

@section(Put in order)

List items in their right order; the student sees them shuffled and gives each its position.

Order the planets from the Sun: @q(order, Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars)

@b{Points.} pts= sets the points; each item in the right place earns its share.

@q(order, pts=4, boil water, add pasta, drain, serve)

@b{Graded.} Each item in its right position earns its share.

@section(Grid and truth-table answers)

Ask for a grid of answers, such as a matrix, a truth table or a K-map, with each cell marked on its own.

Identity matrix: @q(m: 2,2: _1, _0, _0, _1)

@b{Given, graded and empty cells.} =x is a label you give, _x is a blank whose answer is x, a bare _ is an empty cell nobody grades.

@q(m: 3,3: =A, =B, =AND, =0, =1, _0, =1, =1, _1)

@b{Dropdown cells.} A third section lists choices; every graded cell becomes a dropdown of them.

@q(m: 1,2: 0;1 : _1, _0)

@b{Cell size.} m120,80 makes the grid 120 by 80 pixels.

@q(m120,80: 2,2: _1, _2, _3, _4)

@b{Graded.} Each graded cell is worth an equal share, so a nearly right truth table earns nearly full marks.

@section(Survey scales)

Ask how much someone agrees, on a scale; it records the answer and is never marked right or wrong.

This lab was useful. @q(likert5)

@b{Your own words.} labels= gives the scale's words, separated by |, and sets how many points there are.

I read the notes before class. @q(likert5, labels=Never|Rarely|Sometimes|Often|Always)

@b{A longer scale.} scale=7 gives seven points without words.

Rate the pace. @q(likert5, scale=7)

@b{Graded.} Not graded: the answer is recorded with the hand-in and earns no points.

@section(Written answers)

Give the student a box to write a paragraph or more; you mark it by hand, with a rubric if you like.

Explain why the sky is blue. @q(essay)

@b{Starter text.} Text after the colon starts in the box, and the student edits it.

Describe your method. @q(essay: First I measured...)

@b{A rubric.} @@rubric after the question lists criteria with points; you see it while marking, and the grader can suggest a mark.

Explain Ohm's law. @q(essay, pts=4)
@rubric
states V = IR: 2
names the units: 2
@end

@b{Graded.} Marked by a person.

@section(Drawing answers)

Let the student answer by drawing: a sketch on a blank canvas, marks over a picture, or changes to a drawing you start.

Sketch the graph of y = x^2. @q(draw)

@b{Over a picture.} markdraw draws on top of src=, for circling or labelling.

Circle the short circuit. @q(markdraw, src=question-widgets.assets/circuit.svg)

@b{Change a drawing.} moddraw starts with strokes you give, which the student edits.

Fix the diagram. @q(moddraw, src=question-widgets.assets/plot.svg)

@b{Graded.} Marked by a person: the drawing is saved with the hand-in for you to look at.

